  "answer": "Mendel’s monohybrid experiment involved studying the inheritance of a single trait, such as seed color in pea plants. He selected pea plants because they have well-defined traits, are easy to cross-pollinate, and produce offspring quickly. In his experiment, he crossed pure-bred pea plants with yellow seeds (YY) and pure-bred pea plants with green seeds (yy). All the offspring in the first generation (F1) had yellow seeds, indicating that the yellow color is dominant. When these F1 plants were self-pollinated, the second generation (F2) showed a 3:1 ratio of yellow to green seeds. This demonstrated the Law of Dominance, where one trait (yellow) masks the other (green) in the F1 generation, but both traits reappear in the F2 generation in a predictable ratio.",
